<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Félix María Calleja</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1753–<a href="/years/1828">1828</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Félix María Calleja del Rey</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Félix María Calleja was a Spanish army officer who led royalist forces against the Mexican insurgents from 1810. He defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-hidalgo-y-costilla">Miguel Hidalgo</a>'s much larger army at the Bridge of Calderón in January 1811 and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-morelos">José María Morelos</a> at Cuautla in 1812. As Viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> from 1813 to 1816 he pursued a harsh counter-insurgency that brought the rebellion close to defeat.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/felix-maria-calleja.jpg" alt="Félix María Calleja" width="320" height="373" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giuseppe Perovani · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Virrey_F%C3%A9lix_Mar%C3%ADa_Calleja.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Calleja was born on 1 November 1753 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina-del-campo">Medina del Campo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. As a captain he accompanied the Count of Revillagigedo to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> in 1789, when the count became viceroy, and went on to command an infantry brigade in the intendancy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-luis-potosi">San Luis Potosí</a>. Under Viceroy Miguel José de Azanza he fought with severity and cruelty to subdue the Indians of the area, and he also fought Anglo-American filibusters encroaching on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>. Ignacio Allende, later a leader of the independence movement, served under him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-hidalgo-y-costilla">Miguel Hidalgo</a>'s Grito de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dolores-hidalgo">Dolores</a> of 16 September 1810 and the insurgent victory at Monte de las Cruces on 30 October, Viceroy Francisco Javier Venegas ordered Calleja, now a brigadier commanding a cavalry division, to the capital's aid. He routed the insurgents at San Jerónimo Aculco on 7 November 1810 and retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanajuato">Guanajuato</a> on 25 November. At the Bridge of Calderón the insurgents were near victory when a grenade ignited a munitions wagon in their camp. His 4,000 troops became the core of the royalist Army of the Center.</p><p>After an unsuccessful 72-day siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-morelos">José María Morelos</a> at Cuautla, Calleja withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>. Named viceroy on 28 January 1813, he took office on 4 March and found the treasury empty, with more than two million pesos owed to the troops. He confiscated the property of the abolished Inquisition, raised a loan of two million pesos from merchants, farmed out the alcabala, reorganised the treasury and restored commerce and the post, building a well-equipped and paid army.</p><p>He exiled many insurgents to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and later to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. The capture and execution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morelos">Morelos</a> on 22 December 1815 seemed to end the insurrection, but it revived with the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicente-guerrero">Vicente Guerrero</a> in the south, and Calleja's rule grew more dictatorial. Liberal royalists blamed his brutal methods, and his tolerance of his commanders' abuses, for causing more rebellion; their complaints reached the Spanish court, and he was relieved on 20 September 1816.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> he was made Conde de Calderón, received the grand crosses of Isabel the Catholic and San Hermenegildo, and became military commander in Andalucía and governor of Cádiz, charged with organising an expeditionary army for America. Taken prisoner by Rafael Riego, whose uprising against Ferdinand VII began the Liberal Restoration of 1820, he was held on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Mallorca</a> until it collapsed, then restored to his rank. He died on 24 July 1828 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, where he was commander.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Félix María Calleja del Rey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>These forces, commanded by royalist officers such as <b>Félix María Calleja</b>, fought the insurgents during the war of independence from 1810, defeating Miguel Hidalgo at the Bridge of Calderón in January 1811.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina-del-campo">Medina del Campo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.31,-4.92&amp;year=1801" aria-label="Medina del Campo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4430 691 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Félix María Calleja"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4880 961h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4880 961h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.31,-4.92&amp;year=1801">What was here in 1801? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
